Step 1. Mission Overview

• The mission requires a robot to collect resources from one or more storage facilities and deliver them to a specified location.
• The process is divided into two phases:
 – Collection Phase: The robot navigates to storages in an order determined by waiting time plus travel path, sends a request, and waits for retrieval.
 – Delivery Phase: The robot (or robots) transports the collected resource(s) to the destination, possibly making multiple runs.
• Contingency conditions include:
 – If the battery drops to 10% during collection, the robot returns to a charging station and reassigns the collection mission.
 – If the battery drops to 30% during delivery, the robot returns the resource to a checkpoint and reassigns the remaining delivery.
 – In case the resource cannot be returned to a checkpoint, an alert is triggered and a report is sent to the sector manager.
• Additionally, if multiple items must be collected from different storages, multiple robots may work in parallel.

──────────────────────────────
Step 2. High-Level Goal Decomposition

I have defined the top-level goal (G1) as “Complete Resource Mission.” This goal is of type Achieve because its target condition is that all required resources are delivered to the specified location. Under G1, two major sub-goals are defined:
 – G2: “Collect Resources” (a Perform goal because it involves performing concrete actions).
 – G3: “Deliver Resources” (also a Perform goal).

Each of these sub-goals has a built‐in fallback mechanism to handle battery‐low conditions. For example, in the collection phase (G2) the fallback branch is “Handle Low Battery in Collection,” and in the delivery phase (G3) the fallback branch is “Handle Low Battery in Delivery.”

The runtime annotations use the FALLBACK operator to denote that if the primary branch fails (e.g. due to low battery), an alternate plan is executed. Since collection must be completed before delivery, G1 is annotated with sequential (“;”) execution.

